import{_ as a,P as i,d as n,e as r,x as o,q as s,f as c}from"./chunks/framework.BRr8VP3a.js";const g=JSON.parse(`{"title":"A.7 HV07 Feedback Write-Back (Full Interface Card)","description":"","frontmatter":{"title":"A.7 HV07 Feedback Write-Back (Full Interface Card)","xkChars":0,"xkWords":1400,"xkLessons":{"A.7":{"title":"A.7 HV07 Feedback write-back (complete interface card)","lead":"HV07 closes receipt, updating, execution, feedback effects, and learning separately; effective write-back does not automatically mean correct correction or comprehensive improvement.","explanation":["Source location: Chinese source lines 4050–4115; original site location /theory/appendix-a/a-07#section-a-7. See the theory-v2 source for the current full text; historical research drafts and excerpts remain available for checking in their original downloadable versions.","Short quotation translated from Chinese: “A single write-back is not called learning, and write-back does not equal correct correction.” HV07 distinguishes whether feedback arrived, fields changed, changes were executed, and whether they were subsequently retained, invoked, and produced task-level effects. Appeals, audits, or feedback count as institutional write-back in this interface only when they change records, rules, resources, roles, responsibilities, memory, or stopping conditions and are actually executed. [T-main；T-H3]","HV07-R0-writeback-classification checks inputs, receipts, versions, execution, taking effect, persistence, and stopping/rollback statuses under D2 and H3, distinguishing not submitted, submitted, accepted, fields changed, executed, persisting, or failed. Where receipt or statements produce no update, what pauses is the assertion that “institutional write-back is established,” not the value of having been safely heard. [T-main；T-D2]","A procedure that reasonably maintains a decision may also have value; every appeal should not be forced to change its outcome merely to raise reversal rates. Separately check response, understanding of evidence, correction of errors, and restoration of resources and rights. Write-back may itself be wrong, increase harm, or become biased through favorable feedback; effectiveness and content justification require separate judgments. [T-main；T-state]","HV07-R1-causal-feedback requires an eligible G2 to show that the return channel changes preselected subsequent states/transitions relative to no return or a blocked return, adding CAUSAL/E4. It registers feedback and write-back effects within specified fields, channels, and windows; a return path’s existence cannot imply long-term repair, correct goals, or expanded authorization. [T-main；T-G2]","HV07-R2-feedback-mediated-learning requires G2 for effective feedback and G3 support that retainable updates provide a historical conditional increment beyond the current representation for the predetermined task across repeated rounds. Its conclusion reaches only a candidate for learning through historical increment within the task, rounds, and window; it cannot claim that the institution as a whole has learned, its value direction is correct, or comprehensive repair is complete. [T-main; T-history]","HV07-R3-carried-learning is another route: effective feedback already has G2, then C3-carried-learning and §4.5 are used to check the current update carrier, retention across rounds, actual invocation, predetermined-task comparison, and causal grounds. An immediate-feedback instance does not automatically cover subsequent outcomes. It supports a candidate for carrier-transmitted learning within the specified carrier, task, and rounds, without obtaining G3/CM-LEARNING-history status; but lack of G3 support does not cancel this independently supported route either. [T-main; T-history; T-learning]","Training, archiving, publication, or one success alone is insufficient to count as learning; general learning, cross-task transfer, and net benefits should also be resolved separately. An update that is saved but never invoked lacks grounds for learning; if an update is invoked but increases the target error rate, record this according to the predetermined-task comparison, rather than declaring improvement because “the system did change.” [T-main]","The nine axes record cross-level feedback paths and resolution loss. Individual cases do not automatically represent the population; non-submission may arise from lack of digital access, safety threats, or protective anonymity. Silence cannot mean consent, and receipt counts cannot establish coverage of all positions. Separate responsibilities of recipients, decision-makers, write-back implementers, and overseers, with status, deadlines, and results open to independent review. [T-main]","When rollback is authorized, revoke erroneous updates and actually restore the corresponding records, rules, resources, roles, or stop conditions, preserving versions and completion verification. The module generates only needs for review/procedural repair; it does not itself change records, close appeals, or execute repairs, and protective needs do not automatically expand power. [T-main]","The Robodebt website explicitly records the report’s publication and corrected-version dates, and lists recommendations on frontline feedback and automated review. Version notices, recommendation texts, institutional implementation, and actual remedies for residents are different materials. This topic reads only the website, without comparing the PDFs or investigating implementation, so correction notices and published recommendations are not described as learning by the entire institution. [P-ROBO]","Field-by-field study of all 39 fields (“Original card” below retains the field’s complete content in translation; “Interpretation” specifies its role and irreplaceable responsibility).","01 | Interface ID (id). Original card, translated: HV07. Explanation: this is a short identifier within the card collection, tracking the same interface; it does not indicate that a real object has been established. [T-main]","02 | Qualified ID (qualified_id). Original card, translated: human_variable:HV07. Explanation: the qualified namespace prevents name collisions; references to this ID must still carry the object and evidence status. [T-main]","03 | Name (name). Original card, translated: Feedback Write-Back. Explanation: the name provides a reading entry point, not an automatic label for specific people, groups, or real structures. [T-main]","04 | Claim type (claim_type). Original card: H. Interpretation: H identifies the role of a human-instance/interface proposition; it cannot be upgraded into formal proof or an unconditional law of the world. [T-main]","05 | Contract role (contract_role). Original card: human_variable_interface. Interpretation: this variable-interface contract specifies how to enter analysis; a field’s existence does not mean an empirical instance has been established. [T-main]","06 | Proposition (proposition). Original card, translated: Appeals, audits, and feedback constitute human institutional write-back only when they change records, rules, resources, roles, responsibilities, memory, or stop conditions and the corresponding changes have actually been executed. Explanation: this proposition must be understood together with the conditional routes and ceilings below; unconditional causal or normative conclusions cannot be extracted from one sentence. [T-main]","07 | Scope (scope). Original card, translated: Human structures with feedback, appeal, audit, or governance procedures. Explanation: the scope lists task domains that may be examined; it does not mean all objects within it have empirical support. [T-main]","08 | Pause condition (pause_condition). Original card, translated: When there are only receipts, statements, or publication without a state update, or field changes have not yet actually been executed, pause claims that institutional write-back holds; bounded states such as submitted, accepted, and fields changed remain classified under R0. Explanation: with only receipts, statements, publication, or unexecuted field changes, pause claims of institutional write-back while retaining bounded R0 achievements such as submission and acceptance. [T-main]","09 | Inferential dependencies (inferential_requires). Original card, translated: 1. D2. Explanation: inferential dependencies supply the upstream content required for a proposition to hold; upstream candidate/supported status must be passed unchanged, and citation does not automatically elevate it. [T-main]","10 | Protocol dependencies (protocol_requires). Original card, translated from Chinese: 1. EVIDENCE; 2. SOURCE. Interpretation: Protocol dependencies constrain evidence collection and expression; completing protocols does not independently establish the truth of empirical propositions. [T-main]","11 | Qualification/specialization (specializes). Original card, translated: 1. H3. Explanation: H3 is a classification rule for institutional write-back, classifying by the point actually completed; it is not itself evidence of empirical effects, still less a requirement that every valuable appeal change the original decision. [T-main]","12 | Applicable-object references (applies_to). Original card: None (empty set). Interpretation: applicable-object references cannot replace the current object contract; an empty set is explicit registration, not permission to apply to every object. [T-main]","13 | Conditional support routes (conditional_support_routes). Original card, translated: 1. route_id=HV07-R0-writeback-classification;; claim_level=descriptive_classification;; when=Input channels, receipts, before-and-after field versions, execution records, effective times, durations, and stop or rollback states can be checked separately.;; additional_inferential_requires=None (empty set);; additional_protocol_requires=None (empty set);; allowed_conclusion=Distinguish write-back states of not submitted, submitted, accepted, fields changed, executed, persisting, or no longer effective.;; result_ceiling=Only fields changed and actually executed count as institutional write-back; one write-back does not count as learning, nor does write-back equal correct error correction. The procedural value of a reasoned decision to maintain the decision and other changes that have occurred are retained according to their respective evidence.; 2. route_id=HV07-R1-causal-feedback;; claim_level=mechanism_explanation;; when=A qualified G2-instance shows that an institutional return channel changes a preselected subsequent state or transition relative to absent or blocked return conditions.;; additional_inferential_requires=G2-instance;; additional_protocol_requires=CAUSAL, E4;; allowed_conclusion=Register effective feedback and institutional write-back effects within the specified fields, channels, and window.;; result_ceiling=The existence of feedback must not imply learning, long-term repair, justification, or expanded authorization.; 3. route_id=HV07-R2-feedback-mediated-learning;; claim_level=intertemporal_explanation;; when=Effective feedback already has G2-instance support, and a G3-instance shows that retainable updates provide a historical conditional increment for the predetermined task across repeated rounds.;; additional_inferential_requires=G2-instance, G3-instance;; additional_protocol_requires=CAUSAL, E4;; allowed_conclusion=Register a candidate for feedback-mediated learning through historical increment beyond the current state within the specified task, rounds, and window.;; result_ceiling=Must not claim that the institution as a whole has learned, repair is complete, or the value direction is correct.; 4. route_id=HV07-R3-carried-learning;; claim_level=intertemporal_explanation;; when=Effective feedback already has qualified G2-instance support, and C3-carried-learning and §4.5 are used to verify the current update carrier, retention and actual invocation across rounds, predetermined-task comparison, and its causal grounds; an immediate-feedback instance does not automatically cover subsequent task outcomes.;; additional_inferential_requires=G2-instance;; additional_protocol_requires=CAUSAL, E4;; allowed_conclusion=Register a candidate for carrier-transmitted feedback-mediated learning within the specified update carrier, task, and rounds.;; result_ceiling=Does not obtain CM-LEARNING-history or G3 historical-increment status, does not directly call training, archiving, write-back, or one success learning, and does not claim improvement of the institution as a whole. Explanation: R0 classifies processes; R1 covers G2 return-channel effects; R2 covers G2 + G3 learning through historical increment; R3 covers carrier-based learning through G2 + update-carrier retention and invocation and task comparison. One update must not be directly elevated to learning under any route. [T-main]","14 | Allowed inferences (allowed_inference). Original card, translated: 1. Effective write-back, blocked write-back, and superficial feedback. Explanation: these are permitted directions of conclusion; which is actually reached, and at what strength, remains limited by the selected route and materials. [T-main]","15 | Prohibited leaps (prohibited_leap). Original card, translated: 1. A channel guarantees learning; 2. One update equals long-term repair; 3. Silence equals consent. Explanation: these are common overreaches that cannot be avoided merely by rephrasing; check actual inference and subsequent use, not wording alone. [T-main]","16 | Nine-axis scale profile (scale_profile). Original card, translated: SP=<A,X,T,O,C,R,I,N,J>; A=aggregation level: single feedback items or appeals, write-back cases, feedback populations, acceptance/execution distributions, and aggregation rules; X=spatial scope: submission channels, organizational or platform boundaries, institutional jurisdictions, and cross-domain appeal scope; T=time span: submission, acceptance, field change, execution, persistence, and review lags; O=organizational level: feedback roles, receiving teams, organizations, institutions, through to governance ecosystems; C=causal level: feedback events, write-back interaction mechanisms, mesoscopic procedural structures, institutional rules, and system conditions; R=observation resolution: original feedback, handling sequences, write-back cases, outcome distributions, timeliness indicators, and summaries, recording compression losses; I=influence scope: direct appellants and those taking up their appeals, indirectly affected people, second-order institutional consequences, cross-domain and intergenerational effects; N=network topology scope: feedback channels, receiving nodes, review paths, blockages, and cross-level connections; J=jurisdiction and authorization scope: register authorization separately for acceptance, field modification, execution, stopping, rollback, and remedy; register cross-level paths. Explanation: register all nine axes individually; in particular, influence scope I does not produce jurisdictional permission J, and compression losses must be retained when observation resolution R decreases. [T-main]","17 | Effective object (effective_object). Original card, translated: Return channels that change subsequent institutional states or transitions. Explanation: this describes the object corresponding to the interface; when choosing a low-threshold descriptive route, the functional meaning in its name cannot be treated as established causation. [T-main]","18 | Scale invariants (scale_invariants). Original card, translated: 1. Feedback sources, channels, write-back fields, and subsequent changes. Explanation: invariants are comparative responsibilities to preserve during transfer, not a claim that variable values remain unchanged at every scale. [T-main]","19 | Required scale additions (required_scale_additions). Original card, translated: 1. Feedback representativeness; 2. Cross-level write-back paths; 3. Aggregation losses; 4. External review. Explanation: elevation is not copying and enlarging the original table. When materials about new populations, interfaces, responsibility positions, and so on are incomplete, limit conclusions dependent on them. [T-main]","20 | Changing semantics (changing_semantics). Original card, translated: 1. Write-back carriers, lags, and responsible parties may change. Explanation: the same word may change meaning when the object or scale changes; rebuild correspondences rather than assuming the original meaning applies unchanged. [T-main]","21 | Non-applicable objects (non_applicable_objects). Original card, translated: 1. Processes without records, rules, resources, roles, or stop conditions. Explanation: non-applicability constraints prevent unconditional projection of human normative, responsibility, and experience interfaces; they do not deny that other theories can study these objects. [T-main]","22 | Forbidden elevation (forbidden_elevation). Original card, translated: 1. Feedback from individual cases directly represents the population’s opinions. Explanation: inference from local to whole needs separate support; broad influence, grand names, or formal similarity cannot supply missing representativeness or authorization. [T-main]","23 | State set (state). Original card, translated: 1. Arrived; 2. Accepted; 3. Written back; 4. Blocked; 5. Distorted. Explanation: states are descriptive classifications requiring evidence, not a maturity ladder for scoring people; state changes require a window, grounds, and version. [T-main]","24 | Observables (observables). Original card, translated: 1. Proof of submission and acceptance of feedback or appeals; 2. Version differences in records, rules, resources, roles, responsibilities, or stop conditions; 3. Execution records, effective times, and durations of changes; 4. Review, revocation, remedy, and subsequent state changes. Explanation: observable leads indicate where to seek materials; a single lead is not proof of a complete mechanism or causal effect. [T-main]","25 | Evidence requirements (evidence). Original card, translated: 1. Original feedback; 2. Acceptance trail; 3. Field versions; 4. Subsequent rule or resource changes. Explanation: obtain evidence according to route and task relevance; distinguish unknown, uncollected, not applicable, and unavailable for protection, without forcing disclosure to fill the table. [T-main]","26 | Input dependencies and interface contents (input_dependencies). Original card, translated: 1. Feedback sources; 2. Safe channels; 3. Responsible persons; 4. Review procedures. Explanation: this specifies what comes from upstream and its qualifications; gaps cannot silently disappear during transfer between interfaces. [T-main]","27 | Output effects and variable flows (output_effects). Original card, translated: 1. Updates to records, rules, resources, roles, responsibilities, memory, or stop conditions. Explanation: the original card lists update types; which field actually changed and was executed, and when it took effect, require versions and external states. Listing seven types does not mean all were updated. [T-main]","28 | Time window and lag (time_window_and_lag). Original card, translated: Register deadlines for submission, acceptance, decision, execution, and review. Explanation: occurrence, observation, execution, and arrival of effects may happen at different times; short-term materials cannot automatically certify extension beyond the window. [T-main]","29 | Uncertainty (uncertainty). Original card, translated: Record undelivered feedback, protective anonymity, and invisible handling. Explanation: tie uncertainty to specific materials and positions; a single overall confidence score must not conceal positions that were never covered. [T-main]","30 | Local exclusion zone (local_exclusion_zone). Original card, translated: Positions unable to submit safely, facing threats concerning retaliation, or lacking digital access. Explanation: exclusion zones limit the coverage of current inferences; they do not erase these people or facts from the world, and safety-related unavailability does not mean consent or absence of problems. [T-main]","31 | Affected positions (affected_positions). Original card, translated: 1. Submitters; 2. Those evaluated; 3. Implementers; 4. Institutional beneficiaries. Explanation: register both directly and indirectly affected people; visible members do not represent everyone bearing costs. [T-main]","32 | Uptake carriers (carrier). Original card, translated: 1. Appeal systems; 2. Audit procedures; 3. Meeting records; 4. Rule repositories; 5. Responsibility chains. Explanation: carriers are the structures or processes actually carrying something; the carrier’s name, completed construction, and delivery of required functions are different evidence. [T-main]","33 | Responsible parties (responsible_subject). Original card, translated: 1. Recipients; 2. Decision-makers; 3. Write-back implementers; 4. Overseers. Explanation: specify who proposes the judgment and who operates or uses it; registering responsibility does not automatically adjudicate legal liability or issue new authorization. [T-main]","34 | Normative status (normative_status). Original card, translated: Assess feedback effectiveness and justification of feedback content separately. Explanation: an effective channel may return erroneous or unjustified content. Whether errors are correctly corrected, people are protected, and overall net benefits arise cannot be inferred directly from the fact of write-back. [T-main]","35 | Judgment ceiling (judgment_ceiling). Original card, translated: R0 judges the actual point of completion in the write-back process; R1 judges return-channel effects with corresponding G2 support; R3 judges carrier-based feedback-mediated learning through update retention, invocation across rounds, and task comparison; R2 judges learning through historical increment only with additional G3. One write-back, general learning, transfer, and net benefits are resolved separately. Explanation: completion points, channel effects, carrier-based learning, and learning through historical increment are separate; general learning, transfer, and net benefits do not share a resolution either. [T-main]","36 | Action ceiling (action_ceiling). Original card, translated: This variable generates only descriptions of acceptance, field changes, execution, duration, and write-back gaps, and needs for review or procedural repair; it does not authorize rewriting records or rules, executing repairs, or closing appeals. Any real-world adjustment must separately pass C12, explicit runtime N premises, J authorization, and O procedures. Explanation: the interface itself produces no real-world authority to act; actual actions require separate checks of choice, C12, explicit N, valid J, O, protections, and time limits. [T-main]","37 | Counterexamples (counterexamples). Original card, translated: 1. An appeal receives an acknowledgment, but records, rules, resources, and stop conditions are all unchanged; 2. An audit report is published without a responsible person, deadline, or subsequent state update. Explanation: use counterexamples to limit unconditional extrapolation; they may require narrowing scope or collecting evidence again, but do not automatically invalidate every bounded use. [T-main]","38 | Appeal (appeal). Original card, translated: Under appeal_and_rollback_rule, feedback submitters may request status, deadlines, responsible persons, and write-back results through safely accessible anti-retaliation channels, and trigger review independent of the original acceptance, write-back, or decision chain. Explanation: appeals must be safely accessible, free of retaliation, and able to trigger independent review; a name alone does not mean there is power to correct errors. [T-main]","39 | Rollback (rollback). Original card, translated: Under appeal_and_rollback_rule, when rollback is authorized, a designated responsible person must revoke erroneous updates within the specified time, actually restore previous states of records, rules, resources, roles, or stop conditions, and preserve versions and completion verification. Explanation: within valid authorization, actually revoke erroneous effects, correct downstream consequences, and verify completion; changing document wording does not mean rights and resources have been restored to the person concerned. [T-main]"],"argumentSteps":["Under R0, check inputs, acceptance, field versions, actual execution, and persistence.","Retain the procedural value of being heard and of a reasoned decision to maintain a decision; do not demand reversal.","R1 uses G2 to compare subsequent states under return and blockage.","Learning requires separate checks of updates across rounds: R2 checks G3 historical increment beyond the current representation, while R3 checks retention and invocation of current carriers and task comparison.","Evaluate general learning, transfer, net benefits, and repair separately.","Protect people who have not entered safely and allow independent review; after authorization, actually roll back erroneous updates."],"distinctions":[{"leftTitle":"Accepted","leftText":"Feedback has been formally received.","rightTitle":"Institutional write-back","rightText":"Fields changed and actually executed."},{"leftTitle":"Write-back occurred","leftText":"The system state changed.","rightTitle":"Correct error correction","rightText":"Still requires facts, norms, and delivery of recovery."},{"leftTitle":"R2 historical learning","leftText":"Historical increment beyond the current representation.","rightTitle":"R3 carrier-based learning","rightText":"Retention and invocation of current updates and predetermined-task comparison."},{"leftTitle":"One success","leftText":"An outcome in one task window.","rightTitle":"Transfer and net benefits","rightText":"Cross-task performance, costs, and side effects require separate testing."}],"boundaries":["Counterexample in the original card: an appeal has a receipt but records, rules, resources, and stop conditions are all unchanged.","Counterexample in the original card: an audit is published without a responsible person, deadline, or subsequent update.","Teaching counterexample: arbitrarily changing correct decisions to raise reversal rates cannot be called better error correction.","Boundary in the original: lack of G3 does not invalidate independently supported R3 carrier-transmitted learning."],"questions":["Are appeals that do not change the original decision entirely valueless?","Can one write-back establish that the institution has learned?","Does R3 automatically obtain G3 status?","Is effective feedback necessarily correct?"],"diagramPath":"/reading-assets/S-A-7/figures/hv07-status.png","caseLinks":[{"title":"Robodebt report publication, correction notice, and reform recommendations","href":"https://robodebt.royalcommission.gov.au/publications/report"}],"caseNotes":[{"title":"Robodebt report publication, correction notice, and reform recommendations","text":"The official website records the report of July 7, 2023, and corrected version of July 11, and lists recommendations on frontline feedback and automated review.","sourceLabel":"Royal Commission into the Robodebt Scheme: official Report webpage","sourceUrl":"https://robodebt.royalcommission.gov.au/publications/report","sourceLocator":"Report version note (webpage lines 29–54) and recommendations 13.1–13.2 and 17.1 (lines 101–107 and 149–156)","boundary":"Establishes only the website’s version notes and recommendation contents; item-by-item PDF differences, reform implementation, and actual remedies were not checked, so it cannot establish that the whole institution has learned.","href":"https://robodebt.royalcommission.gov.au/publications/report"}],"sourceRefs":[{"section":"A.7","start_line":4143,"end_line":4208,"startLine":4143,"endLine":4208}],"outputId":"section-a-7","sourceHash":"51795bb7988f2669a324a52666c320cfba1382f597f7b5c6a1431844572da1fb","teachingExamples":[{"title":"Teaching construction: four states do not share a single completion marker","text":"A constructed appeal is submitted on Monday, accepted on Tuesday, has fields modified on Thursday, and only the following Monday has resource restoration actually executed. Tuesday may be recorded as accepted, not resources restored; Thursday’s field changes also do not establish execution. Even after the corresponding restoration arrives the following Monday, one case does not establish long-term learning by the entire institution or positive net benefits.","assumptions":"The timeline and resource records are fictional.","boundary":"Actual restoration and authorization require independent evidence; the bounded value of hearing and acceptance themselves is not denied."},{"title":"Teaching construction: retained updates must still be invoked and compared","text":"A constructed program saves a correction rule in version v2 but still runs v1 for the next three rounds. Archiving does not prove that v2 produced learning effects. Even if v2 is actually invoked for three later rounds, predefined task comparisons and causal materials remain necessary. Any historical increment beyond the current representation additionally requires G3 testing; invocation counts are not universal proof.","assumptions":"Program versions and rounds are constructed.","boundary":"R3 requires no additional G3, but does require its own retention, invocation, and task comparison; R2 separately bears responsibility for historical increments."}],"counterexamples":[{"identity":"source_counterexample","text":"Counterexample in the original card: an appeal has a receipt but records, rules, resources, and stop conditions are all unchanged.","effect":"Limits unconditional extension of this section without negating independently grounded bounded judgments.","sourceIds":["T-main"]},{"identity":"source_counterexample","text":"Counterexample in the original card: an audit is published without a responsible person, deadline, or subsequent update.","effect":"Limits unconditional extension of this section without negating independently grounded bounded judgments.","sourceIds":["T-main"]},{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"Teaching counterexample: arbitrarily changing correct decisions to raise reversal rates cannot be called better error correction.","effect":"Limits unconditional extension of this section without negating independently grounded bounded judgments.","sourceIds":["T-main"]},{"identity":"source_counterexample","text":"Boundary in the original: lack of G3 does not invalidate independently supported R3 carrier-transmitted learning.","effect":"Limits unconditional extension of this section without negating independently grounded bounded judgments.","sourceIds":["T-main"]}],"questionAnswers":[{"question":"Are appeals that do not change the original decision entirely valueless?","answer":"No. Reasoned maintenance, hearing, and other actual changes remain according to independent evidence.","basis":"R0 inference ceiling."},{"question":"Can one write-back establish that the institution has learned?","answer":"No; retention and invocation across rounds, task comparisons, and the corresponding routes require separate checks.","basis":"R2/R3。"},{"question":"Does R3 automatically obtain G3 status?","answer":"No. Carrier-mediated transmission is not a historical increment beyond the current representation.","basis":"R3 ceiling."},{"question":"Is effective feedback necessarily correct?","answer":"No. Content justification and correct correction are judged separately.","basis":"normative_status。"}],"figures":[{"path":"/reading-assets/S-A-7/figures/hv07-status.png","alt":"Record submission/acceptance, field changes, actual execution, persistence, or failure separately; institutional write-back is not automatically correct correction.","caption":"Based on A.7 R0 and the original state fields."},{"path":"/reading-assets/S-A-7/figures/hv07-routes.png","alt":"R0 classification; R1 G2 feedback; R2 G2 plus G3 historical learning; R3 G2 plus retention, invocation, and task comparisons through current carriers: do not conflate them.","caption":"Based on the four routes in A.7."},{"path":"/reading-assets/S-A-7/figures/hv07-learning.png","alt":"Gather evidence separately for one update, learning in general, transfer across tasks, and net benefit; saving is not invocation, and invocation is not benefit.","caption":"Based on judgment_ceiling in A.7."}],"diagramBrief":"The first diagram distinguishes completion positions, the second lists four routes, and the third separates learning, transfer, and net benefit. Processes may be interrupted; stages that never happened cannot be marked complete merely for completeness.","applicability":{"realCase":"applicable","diagram":"applicable","reason":"This unit concerns methodological judgment. Actual sources support only the listed facts; the diagram and teaching construction each retain their own status."},"openQuestions":["No real G2/G3 or carrier-learning instance was established; institutional improvement is not certified.","Reasoned maintenance of particular appeal decisions, correction reaching its destination, residual harm, people who never submitted, and long-term net benefit require separate investigations."],"taskId":"S-A-7","sources":[{"id":"T-main","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari, On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68; the text dates itself to October 2026","locator":"§A.7, Chinese source lines 3964–4029","claim_support":["All 39 HV07 fields, four routes, and rules for separate closure."],"limits":["Supports the current manuscript's textual meaning and explicitly bounded inference; does not by itself establish empirical universality, implementation effects or readers' learning outcomes.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v2."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"T-H3","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari, On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68; the text dates itself to October 2026","locator":"§7.1, Chinese source lines 1469–1485","claim_support":["The role of H3 classification rules."],"limits":["Supports the current manuscript's textual meaning and explicitly bounded inference; does not by itself establish empirical universality, implementation effects or readers' learning outcomes.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v2."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"T-D2","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari, On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68; the text dates itself to October 2026","locator":"§3.3, Chinese source lines 463–472","claim_support":["D2 feedback definition."],"limits":["Supports the current manuscript's textual meaning and explicitly bounded inference; does not by itself establish empirical universality, implementation effects or readers' learning outcomes.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v2."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"T-G2","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari, On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68; the text dates itself to October 2026","locator":"§4.4, Chinese source lines 610–619","claim_support":["Return-channel effects."],"limits":["Supports the current manuscript's textual meaning and explicitly bounded inference; does not by itself establish empirical universality, implementation effects or readers' learning outcomes.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v2."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"T-history","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari, On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68; the text dates itself to October 2026","locator":"§4.5, Chinese source lines 620–641","claim_support":["Historical increments and the current-carrier route."],"limits":["Supports the current manuscript's textual meaning and explicitly bounded inference; does not by itself establish empirical universality, implementation effects or readers' learning outcomes.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v2."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"T-learning","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari, On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68; the text dates itself to October 2026","locator":"§4.11.3, Chinese source lines 744–765","claim_support":["C3 feedback/learning conditions."],"limits":["Supports the current manuscript's textual meaning and explicitly bounded inference; does not by itself establish empirical universality, implementation effects or readers' learning outcomes.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v2."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"T-state","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari, On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68; the text dates itself to October 2026","locator":"§14.7, Chinese source lines 2954–2967","claim_support":["Four correction endpoints and grounded restoration."],"limits":["Supports the current manuscript's textual meaning and explicitly bounded inference; does not by itself establish empirical universality, implementation effects or readers' learning outcomes.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v2."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"P-ROBO","title":"Royal Commission into the Robodebt Scheme: official Report webpage","url":"https://robodebt.royalcommission.gov.au/publications/report","source_type":"primary","version_or_date":"Report dated 2023-07-07; corrected-version notice dated 07-11","locator":"Report version note (webpage lines 29–54) and recommendations 13.1–13.2 and 17.1 (lines 101–107 and 149–156)","claim_support":["The official site records publication on July 7 and correction on July 11; the recommendations list calls for frontline feedback and review of automated decisions."],"limits":["The researcher read the official list and version notice, without item-by-item comparison of the two PDFs or verification of implementation. Report publication is not equated with institutional repair or learning."],"access_status":"partially_read"},{"id":"theory-v2","title":"Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, v2, November 2026","url":"/downloads/Xikari-条件结构与转化论.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"v2 / 2026-11-16","locator":"A.7 HV07 Feedback write-back (complete interface card); Chinese source lines 4050–4115; SHA-256 6ef2e508474d4ac1ad07d7e4e068955342c405b5f680afcfc40d68cca3d0149c","claim_support":["The current source section and this text’s conceptual explanation of v2; not a substitute for external empirical materials."],"limits":["Older research attachments retain their original manuscript identity; the previous scope of external reading has not expanded because of this edition change."],"access_status":"read"}],"lecturePath":"/reading-assets/S-A-7/lecture.md","sourcesPath":"/reading-assets/S-A-7/sources.json","sourceSlicePath":"/reading-assets/S-A-7/source/S-A-7.md","supplementaryFiles":[],"diagramsPath":"/reading-assets/S-A-7/diagrams.md","editionNote":"This page’s source text and section locations correspond to v2; historical research attachments and prior external-reading records retain their own versions and scopes.","translationOf":{"language":"zh","lesson_sha256":"60b52b64550ae39de2be7ec4a298c5e8adbd227142d6ebdfc5b222b0386de97f","source_sha256":"6ef2e508474d4ac1ad07d7e4e068955342c405b5f680afcfc40d68cca3d0149c"}}}},"headers":[],"relativePath":"en/theory/appendix-a/a-07.md","filePath":"en/theory/appendix-a/a-07.md"}`),d={name:"en/theory/appendix-a/a-07.md"};function l(u,e,p,h,f,m){const t=i("SectionLesson");return n(),r("div",null,[e[0]||(e[0]=o('<div class="theory-source"><h1 id="section-a-7" tabindex="-1"><span class="xk-num"><span class="xk-num__n">A.7</span><span class="xk-num__gap"></span></span>HV07 Feedback Write-Back (Full Interface Card) <a class="header-anchor" href="#section-a-7" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;A.7 HV07 Feedback Write-Back (Full Interface Card) {#section-a-7}&quot;">​</a></h1><p>A. Identity, Proposition, and Scope</p><div class="xk-table"><table><thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>What to Record</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Interface ID (<code>id</code>)</td><td>HV07</td></tr><tr><td>Qualified ID (<code>qualified_id</code>)</td><td><code>human_variable:HV07</code></td></tr><tr><td>Name (<code>name</code>)</td><td>Feedback Write-Back</td></tr><tr><td>Claim type (<code>claim_type</code>)</td><td>H</td></tr><tr><td>Contract role (<code>contract_role</code>)</td><td><code>human_variable_interface</code></td></tr><tr><td>Proposition (<code>proposition</code>)</td><td>An appeal, audit, or piece of feedback constitutes institutional write-back in a human structure only if it changes records, rules, resources, roles, responsibilities, memory, or stopping conditions and the corresponding change has actually been executed.</td></tr><tr><td>Scope (<code>scope</code>)</td><td>Human structures with feedback, appeals, audits, or governance procedures</td></tr><tr><td>Conditions for suspension (<code>pause_condition</code>)</td><td>Suspend the claim of institutional write-back if there is no state update and only a receipt, statement of position, or publication, or if field changes have not actually been executed. Limited statuses such as submitted, intake complete, and fields changed are still classified under R0.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>B. Formal Dependencies and Limits on Conclusions</p><div class="xk-table"><table><thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>What to Record</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Inferential dependencies (<code>inferential_requires</code>)</td><td>1. D2</td></tr><tr><td>Protocol dependencies (<code>protocol_requires</code>)</td><td>1. EVIDENCE<br>2. SOURCE</td></tr><tr><td>Qualification/specialization (<code>specializes</code>)</td><td>1. H3</td></tr><tr><td>References to applicable objects (<code>applies_to</code>)</td><td>None (empty set)</td></tr><tr><td>Conditional support routes (<code>conditional_support_routes</code>)</td><td>1. route_id=HV07-R0-writeback-classification;<br>claim_level=descriptive_classification;<br>when=Input channels, receipts, field versions before and after, execution records, time of entry into effect, duration, and stopping or rollback status can be checked separately;<br>additional_inferential_requires=None (empty set);<br>additional_protocol_requires=None (empty set);<br>allowed_conclusion=Distinguish the write-back statuses of not submitted, submitted, intake complete, fields changed, executed, continuing, or no longer in effect;<br>result_ceiling=Only field changes that are actually executed count as institutional write-back. A single write-back does not count as learning, and write-back does not amount to successful correction. Retain the procedural value of upholding a decision for reasons, and any other changes that have occurred, according to their own evidence.<br>2. route_id=HV07-R1-causal-feedback;<br>claim_level=mechanism_explanation;<br>when=An eligible G2-instance shows that an institutional return channel changes the preselected subsequent state or transition relative to a condition without a return or with the channel blocked;<br>additional_inferential_requires=G2-instance;<br>additional_protocol_requires=CAUSAL, E4;<br>allowed_conclusion=Register effective feedback and effects of institutional write-back within the specified fields, channel, and window;<br>result_ceiling=Do not infer learning, long-term repair, normative justification, or broader authorization from the presence of feedback.<br>3. route_id=HV07-R2-feedback-mediated-learning;<br>claim_level=intertemporal_explanation;<br>when=Effective feedback already has support from a G2-instance, and a G3-instance shows that a retainable update provides a historical conditional increment for a predetermined task over repeated rounds;<br>additional_inferential_requires=G2-instance, G3-instance;<br>additional_protocol_requires=CAUSAL, E4;<br>allowed_conclusion=Register a candidate for feedback-mediated learning with a historical increment beyond the current state, limited to the specified task, rounds, and window;<br>result_ceiling=Do not claim that the institution as a whole has learned, that repair is complete, or that its direction is normatively right.<br>4. route_id=HV07-R3-carried-learning;<br>claim_level=intertemporal_explanation;<br>when=An eligible G2-instance already supports effective feedback. The current carrier of the update, retention and actual use across rounds, a comparison for the predetermined task, and the causal basis for that comparison are verified under C3-carried-learning and §4.5. An instance of immediate feedback does not automatically cover later task outcomes;<br>additional_inferential_requires=G2-instance;<br>additional_protocol_requires=CAUSAL, E4;<br>allowed_conclusion=Register a candidate for feedback-mediated learning through carrier transmission within the specified update carrier, task, and rounds;<br>result_ceiling=This does not qualify as a historical increment under CM-LEARNING-history or G3. Do not directly call training, archiving, write-back, or one success learning. Do not claim the institution as a whole has improved.</td></tr><tr><td>Permitted inferences (<code>allowed_inference</code>)</td><td>1. Effective write-back, blocked write-back, and feedback in appearance only</td></tr><tr><td>Prohibited leaps (<code>prohibited_leap</code>)</td><td>1. Inferring learning from the presence of a channel<br>2. Treating one update as long-term repair<br>3. Treating silence as consent</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>C. Nine-Axis Scale and Object Contract</p><div class="xk-table"><table><thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>What to Record</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Nine-axis scale profile (<code>scale_profile</code>)</td><td>SP=&lt;A,X,T,O,C,R,I,N,J&gt;. A=Aggregation level covers individual feedback events or appeals, cases of write-back, the feedback population, distributions of intake and execution, and aggregation rules. X=Spatial extent covers submission channels, organizational or platform boundaries, institutional jurisdictions, and the reach of appeals across domains. T=Time span covers submission, intake, field changes, execution, duration, and review lags. O=Organizational level covers feedback roles, intake teams, organizations, institutions, and governance ecologies. C=Causal level covers feedback events, mechanisms of interaction in write-back, meso-level procedural structures, institutional rules, and systemic conditions. R=Resolution covers original feedback, processing sequences, cases of write-back, outcome distributions, timeliness metrics, and summaries. Record compression loss as well. I=Scope of influence covers people directly appealing or taking up the work, indirectly affected people, second-order institutional consequences, and impacts across domains and generations. N=Network topology scope covers feedback channels, intake nodes, review paths, blockages, and connections across levels. J=Scope of jurisdiction and authorization requires separate authorization records for intake, field changes, execution, stopping, rollback, and remedy. Register paths across levels</td></tr><tr><td>Effective object (<code>effective_object</code>)</td><td>A return channel that changes a subsequent institutional state or transition</td></tr><tr><td>Scale invariants (<code>scale_invariants</code>)</td><td>1. Source and channel of feedback, fields written back, and subsequent changes</td></tr><tr><td>Required additions for scale elevation (<code>required_scale_additions</code>)</td><td>1. Representativeness of feedback<br>2. Write-back paths across levels<br>3. Aggregation loss<br>4. External review</td></tr><tr><td>What changes with scale (<code>changing_semantics</code>)</td><td>1. Write-back carriers, lags, and responsible parties can change</td></tr><tr><td>Inapplicable objects (<code>non_applicable_objects</code>)</td><td>1. Processes without records, rules, resources, roles, or stopping conditions</td></tr><tr><td>Prohibited elevations (<code>forbidden_elevation</code>)</td><td>1. Treating feedback from an individual case as directly representative of the population’s views</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>D. State, Evidence, and Variable Flows</p><div class="xk-table"><table><thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>What to Record</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>State set (<code>state</code>)</td><td>1. Arrival<br>2. Intake<br>3. Write-back<br>4. Blockage<br>5. Distortion</td></tr><tr><td>Observables (<code>observables</code>)</td><td>1. Evidence of submission and intake of feedback or an appeal<br>2. Differences between versions of records, rules, resources, roles, responsibilities, or stopping conditions<br>3. Execution records, time of entry into effect, and duration of the change<br>4. Review, reversal, remedy, and subsequent state changes</td></tr><tr><td>Evidence requirements (<code>evidence</code>)</td><td>1. Original feedback<br>2. Intake trail<br>3. Versions of fields<br>4. Subsequent changes to rules or resources</td></tr><tr><td>Input dependencies and interface content (<code>input_dependencies</code>)</td><td>1. Feedback sources<br>2. Safe channels<br>3. Responsible parties<br>4. Review procedures</td></tr><tr><td>Output effects and variable flows (<code>output_effects</code>)</td><td>1. Updates to records, rules, resources, roles, responsibilities, memory, or stopping conditions</td></tr><tr><td>Time window and lag (<code>time_window_and_lag</code>)</td><td>Register time limits for submission, intake, decision, execution, and further review</td></tr><tr><td>Uncertainty (<code>uncertainty</code>)</td><td>Record feedback that did not arrive, anonymity for protection, and handling that cannot be observed</td></tr><tr><td>Local exclusion zone (<code>local_exclusion_zone</code>)</td><td>Positions without safe submission or digital access, or under threat of retaliation</td></tr><tr><td>Affected positions (<code>affected_positions</code>)</td><td>1. People submitting material<br>2. People evaluated<br>3. People carrying out the action<br>4. Beneficiaries of the institution</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>E. Uptake, Responsibility, Normative Status, Ceilings, and Correction</p><div class="xk-table"><table><thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>What to Record</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Uptake carriers (<code>carrier</code>)</td><td>1. Appeal systems<br>2. Audit procedures<br>3. Meeting records<br>4. Repositories of rules<br>5. Chains of responsibility</td></tr><tr><td>Responsible parties (<code>responsible_subject</code>)</td><td>1. Parties responsible for intake<br>2. Decision makers<br>3. Parties who execute write-back<br>4. Parties responsible for oversight</td></tr><tr><td>Normative status (<code>normative_status</code>)</td><td>Whether feedback is effective and whether its contents are normatively justified are judged separately</td></tr><tr><td>Judgment ceiling (<code>judgment_ceiling</code>)</td><td>R0 judges how far the write-back process has actually gone. R1 judges the effect of the return channel with corresponding support from G2. R3 judges feedback-mediated learning through a carrier from retention of the update, its use across rounds, and a task comparison. R2 judges learning with a historical increment only with additional G3. A single write-back, learning in general, transfer, and net benefits are assessed separately.</td></tr><tr><td>Action ceiling (<code>action_ceiling</code>)</td><td>This variable produces only descriptions of intake, field changes, execution, duration, and gaps in write-back, along with needs for review or procedural repair. It does not authorize rewriting records or rules, carrying out repair, or closing appeals. Any real-world adjustment separately requires C12, explicit N premises at runtime, J authorization, and O procedures</td></tr><tr><td>Counterexamples (<code>counterexamples</code>)</td><td>1. An appeal gets a receipt, but records, rules, resources, and stopping conditions all remain unchanged<br>2. An audit report is published without a responsible party, deadline, or subsequent state update</td></tr><tr><td>Appeal (<code>appeal</code>)</td><td>Under <code>appeal_and_rollback_rule</code>, people who submit feedback can request its status, time limit, responsible party, and write-back outcome through a safe, accessible channel with anti-retaliation protection. This triggers review independent of the original intake, write-back, or decision chain</td></tr><tr><td>Rollback (<code>rollback</code>)</td><td>Under <code>appeal_and_rollback_rule</code>, once rollback is authorized, the designated responsible party must within the stated deadline revoke erroneous updates and actually restore the previous states of records, rules, resources, roles, or stopping conditions. Preserve versions and verification of completion</td></tr></tbody></table></div></div>',1)),s(t,{section:"A.7"}),e[1]||(e[1]=c("div",{class:"theory-source"},null,-1))])}const v=a(d,[["render",l]]);export{g as __pageData,v as default};
